	<section class="dh-section dh-wrap" aria-labelledby="h-witz">
		<div class="hp-joke">
			<div class="dh-stack" style="gap:20px">
				<p class="dh-kicker dh-rise">One punchline, different reactions</p>
				<h2 class="dh-h2 dh-rise" id="h-witz">The same joke. Different people. Different reactions.</h2>
				<div class="hp-prose dh-rise">
					<p>A joke can delight one person, leave another cold and irritate a third. That is not just down to the joke itself. Humour preferences, experiences, expectations and the situation also play a part.</p>
					<p>So anyone studying humour and personality asks not only “Is this funny?” but also:</p>
				</div>
				<ul class="hp-asks dh-rise"><li>Is this funny?</li><li>For whom?</li><li>What kind of humour is this?</li><li>How is humour being used here?</li><li>What does the reaction actually tell us?</li></ul>
			</div>
			<figure class="hp-joke-card dh-rise" style="margin:0">
				<p class="dh-kicker">A small example</p>
				<blockquote>What’s orange and sounds like a parrot?<span class="pt">A carrot.</span></blockquote>
				<div class="hp-react">
					<div><span class="face" style="--c: var(--lime)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="9" cy="10" r="1" fill="var(--ink)"/><circle cx="15" cy="10" r="1" fill="var(--ink)"/><path d="M7.5 14.5c1.2 2.2 2.7 3 4.5 3s3.3-.8 4.5-3z" fill="var(--ink)"/></svg></span><p><b>“Brilliant!”</b><span>likes puns and nonsense</span></p></div>
					<div><span class="face" style="--c: var(--paper)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="9" cy="10" r="1" fill="var(--ink)"/><circle cx="15" cy="10" r="1" fill="var(--ink)"/><path d="M8 15.5h8"/></svg></span><p><b>“Hm.”</b><span>finds the punchline too tame</span></p></div>
					<div><span class="face" style="--c: var(--peach)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="9" cy="10" r="1" fill="var(--ink)"/><circle cx="15" cy="10" r="1" fill="var(--ink)"/><path d="M8.5 16c1-1 2.2-1.4 3.5-1.4s2.5.4 3.5 1.4"/></svg></span><p><b>“Seriously?”</b><span>has little time for groan-worthy puns</span></p></div>
				</div>
				<small>Possible reactions, not statistics. None of them reveals a personality type.</small>
			</figure>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="dh-wrap" id="ebenen" aria-labelledby="h-ebenen">
		<div class="hp-head">
			<p class="dh-kicker dh-rise">Three questions that often get mixed up</p>
			<h2 class="dh-h2 dh-rise" id="h-ebenen">What we find funny is not the same as what we do with humour</h2>
		</div>
		<div class="hp-levels">
			<article class="hp-level dh-rise" style="--c: var(--lime)"><span class="n">Level 1</span><h3>Humour preference</h3><p>Which forms of humour appeal to me? For example puns, nonsense, irony, satire or observational humour.</p></article>
			<article class="hp-level dh-rise" style="--c: var(--peach)"><span class="n">Level 2</span><h3>Humour use</h3><p>How do I use humour in everyday life? For example to connect with others, to cope with a difficult situation or to tease people.</p></article>
			<article class="hp-level dh-rise" style="--c: #8f8fe6"><span class="n">Level 3</span><h3>Personality</h3><p>Which broader traits are statistically associated with humour preferences or humour use in studies?</p></article>
		</div>
		<p class="hp-note dh-rise">These questions can be related. But they should not be merged into a single statement about someone’s character.</p>
	</section>

	<section class="dh-section dh-wrap" aria-labelledby="h-stile">
		<div class="hp-styles-wrap">
			<div class="dh-stack" style="gap:20px">
				<p class="dh-kicker dh-rise">Four humour styles: how humour is used in everyday life</p>
				<h2 class="dh-h2 dh-rise" id="h-stile">Martin’s humour styles describe functions of humour</h2>
				<p class="dh-rise">The Humor Styles Questionnaire by Rod Martin and colleagues distinguishes four dimensions that describe how people use humour in everyday life. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q1">Martin et al., 2003</a>)</span> It is not about sorting people into four fixed personality types.</p>
				<p class="dh-rise dh-small dh-muted">The model arranges the styles along two questions: is the humour directed more at others or at oneself? And is it more benevolent or more hurtful? <span class="ref">(<a href="#q1">Martin et al., 2003</a>)</span></p>
			</div>
			<div class="hp-grid dh-rise">
				<span></span><span class="hp-axis">Focus: others</span><span class="hp-axis">Focus: oneself</span>
				<i class="hp-achsen" aria-hidden="true"><b class="x"></b><b class="y"></b></i>
				<span class="hp-axis v">more benevolent</span>
				<article class="hp-style warm"><span class="en">Affiliative</span><h3>Affiliative humour</h3><p>Humour that can support relationships and shared lightness, such as telling things in a joking way, laughing together or easing a tense situation.</p></article>
				<article class="hp-style warm"><span class="en">Self-enhancing</span><h3>Self-enhancing humour</h3><p>A humorous attitude towards your own life and difficult situations. It can help you gain distance or change your perspective.</p></article>
				<span class="hp-axis v">more hurtful</span>
				<article class="hp-style cost"><span class="en">Aggressive</span><h3>Aggressive humour</h3><p>Humour that targets others, for example through mockery, sarcasm or belittling teasing. How it works also depends on relationship, power and situation.</p></article>
				<article class="hp-style cost"><span class="en">Self-defeating</span><h3>Self-defeating humour</h3><p>Making yourself the butt of the humour to entertain others or secure social belonging. Depending on how often and in what context, this can be experienced in different ways.</p></article>
			</div>
		</div>
		<p class="hp-note2 dh-rise"><b>Self-irony is not automatically self-defeating.</b> Joking about the tea you forgot can be playful self-irony. The self-defeating humour style is about a recurring way of making yourself the target at your own expense, for example to gain recognition or belonging. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q1">Martin et al., 2003</a>)</span></p>
		<p class="hp-note dh-rise">People do not use just one style. The dimensions are there to help reflect on typical patterns, not to label people once and for all.</p>
	</section>

	<section class="dh-wrap" aria-labelledby="h-comic">
		<div class="hp-comic hp-dark">
			<div class="dh-stack" style="gap:20px">
				<p class="dh-kicker">Humour preferences: what do people find funny?</p>
				<h2 class="dh-h2" id="h-comic">From puns to nonsense: humour comes in many forms</h2>
				<h3 class="hp-sub">What we find funny</h3>
				<p>Willibald Ruch studied how people rate different joke structures. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q3">Ruch, 1992</a>)</span> Some people like punchlines where everything fits together in the end. Others like nonsense, where a residue of absurdity remains.</p>
				<h3 class="hp-sub">How we express humour</h3>
				<p>Ruch and colleagues also describe eight comic styles, including wit, irony, satire and sarcasm. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q2">Ruch et al., 2018</a>)</span> The comic styles describe different ways of expressing humour. Martin’s four humour styles focus on how people use humour in everyday life, for example for social connection or for coping with difficulties. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q1">Martin et al., 2003</a>)</span></p>
				<p class="hp-warn"><b>Beware of jumping to conclusions</b>Liking absurd humour does not automatically make someone “creative”. Enjoying irony does not automatically make someone cynical. Conclusions like these go beyond the research.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="hp-chips" aria-label="Eight comic styles according to Ruch and colleagues">
				<span style="--c: var(--lime)">Fun</span><span style="--c: var(--lime)">Benevolent humour</span><span style="--c: var(--paper)">Nonsense</span><span style="--c: var(--paper)">Wit</span><span style="--c: #c9c9f5">Irony</span><span style="--c: #c9c9f5">Satire</span><span style="--c: var(--peach)">Sarcasm</span><span style="--c: var(--peach)">Cynicism</span>
				<small>The eight comic styles from the research by Ruch and colleagues. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q2">Ruch et al., 2018</a>)</span> The colours are grouped only for orientation and are not a judgement.</small>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="dh-section dh-wrap" aria-labelledby="h-pers">
		<div class="hp-pers">
			<div class="dh-stack" style="gap:20px">
				<p class="dh-kicker dh-rise">What does personality have to do with it?</p>
				<h2 class="dh-h2 dh-rise" id="h-pers">Associations are interesting, but they are no personality formula</h2>
				<div class="hp-prose dh-rise">
					<p>Studies examine how humour preferences and humour styles relate to personality traits such as the Big Five. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q2">Ruch et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#q4">Plessen et al., 2020</a>)</span> They find statistical relationships, but no simple key that reliably infers an individual’s humour from a personality trait.</p>
					<p>One example: in a Czech study with 509 participants, greater open-mindedness was associated with a relative preference for nonsense over humour with resolvable incongruity. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q6">Current Psychology, 2023</a>)</span> That describes a tendency in this sample.</p>
					<p>The findings depend on, among other things, which kind of humour is measured, which scale is used and which people are studied. That is why a cautious statement makes more sense than a rigid scheme: personality can be related to humour preferences and humour use, but it does not fully determine them.</p>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="hp-big5 dh-rise">
				<b>The Big Five</b>
				<ul>
					<li>Openness to experience<span>curious, imaginative</span></li>
					<li>Conscientiousness<span>organised, reliable</span></li>
					<li>Extraversion<span>sociable, energetic</span></li>
					<li>Agreeableness<span>cooperative, compassionate</span></li>
					<li>Neuroticism<span>emotionally sensitive</span></li>
				</ul>
				<p class="hp-formula"><s>Trait X = humour type Y</s><br>Tendencies at group level, not predictions for individuals.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="dh-wrap" aria-labelledby="h-kontext">
		<div class="hp-meet">
			<div class="dh-rise"><svg class="hp-venn" viewBox="0 0 440 400" role="img" aria-label="Three overlapping circles: person, joke and situation">
<circle cx="160" cy="150" r="118" fill="var(--lime)" opacity=".85"/>
<circle cx="280" cy="150" r="118" fill="var(--peach)" opacity=".75"/>
<circle cx="220" cy="250" r="118" fill="#b7b7f0" opacity=".75"/>
<text x="120" y="120" text-anchor="middle">Person</text>
<text x="320" y="120" text-anchor="middle">Joke</text>
<text x="220" y="320" text-anchor="middle">Situation</text>
<circle cx="220" cy="183" r="8" fill="var(--ink)"/>
</svg></div>
			<div class="dh-stack" style="gap:20px">
				<p class="dh-kicker dh-rise">Humour is where person, joke and situation meet</p>
				<h2 class="dh-h2 dh-rise" id="h-kontext">It is not only style that counts, but also context</h2>
				<p class="dh-rise">How humour comes across depends not only on who someone is. The form of the joke, the relationship between those involved, the topic, the occasion and the power relations also play a part.</p>
				<p class="dh-rise">A person may use a style differently among friends than in a meeting. And the same joke can work very differently depending on the audience.</p>
				<ol class="hp-qs dh-rise">
					<li>What kind of humour is this?</li>
					<li>What function might the humour have in this situation?</li>
					<li>Who is the target of the joke, and who else is listening?</li>
					<li>What relationship and what power relations are involved?</li>
					<li>What reactions are possible, even if they are not intended?</li>
				</ol>
				<div class="dh-actions dh-rise"><a class="dh-btn" href="<?php dehumore_href( '/humor-risk-check/' ); ?>">Assess a specific joke</a></div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="dh-section dh-wrap" id="reflexion" aria-labelledby="h-refl">
		<div class="hp-reflect">
			<div class="hp-reflect-head">
				<div class="dh-stack" style="gap:16px">
					<p class="dh-kicker" style="color:var(--ink)">Your own reflection instead of a type test</p>
					<h2 class="dh-h2" id="h-refl">What role does humour play for you?</h2>
					<p style="max-width:60ch">The following questions are an aid to reflection. They are not a psychological test and do not provide a diagnosis.</p>
				</div>
				<span class="hp-badge">De:Humore reflection aid · not a test</span>
			</div>
			<ol class="hp-cards">
				<li>Which kinds of humour often make you laugh?</li>
				<li>Which forms of humour do you like to share yourself?</li>
				<li>When does humour help you see a situation differently?</li>
				<li>What kind of humour do you use to connect with others?</li>
				<li>Is there humour that makes you uncomfortable or crosses a line for you?</li>
				<li>Does your humour change depending on the group, your role or the situation?</li>
				<li class="wide">What do you want to achieve with your humour in a specific moment?</li>
			</ol>
			<div class="hp-reflect-foot">
				<p>The point is not to put you in a category. The questions are meant to help you become more aware of preferences, habits and possible effects. If you would like to try a different angle:</p>
				<div class="hp-book"><b>Further reading: a practical perspective</b>In <em>Humor, Seriously</em>, Jennifer Aaker and Naomi Bagdonas describe their own down-to-earth model of humour at work. <a href="https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/611544/humor-seriously-by-jennifer-aaker-and-naomi-bagdonas/">To the book</a></div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="dh-wrap" id="organisationen" aria-labelledby="h-org">
		<div class="hp-org">
			<div class="dh-stack" style="gap:20px">
				<p class="dh-kicker dh-rise">Humour and personality in organisations</p>
				<h2 class="dh-h2 dh-rise" id="h-org">Understanding differences, shaping collaboration more consciously</h2>
				<p class="dh-rise">In teams, different humour preferences, experiences and limits come together. That can create connection, but it can also lead to misunderstandings. Making these differences something you can talk about does not mean getting everyone onto the same humour. It can already help to understand what role humour plays in the team and how certain forms might affect different people.</p>
				<div class="dh-actions dh-rise"><a class="dh-btn" href="<?php dehumore_href( '/humor-in-organisationen/' ); ?>">See humour training for organisations</a></div>
			</div>
