					<li>Differences in humour preferences</li>
					<li>Humour, relationships and hierarchy</li>
					<li>Humour in leadership and team communication</li>
					<li>Impact, limits and fit with the situation</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="dh-section dh-wrap" aria-labelledby="h-stand">
		<div class="hp-head">
			<p class="dh-kicker dh-rise">State of research and limits</p>
			<h2 class="dh-h2 dh-rise" id="h-stand">What the research can say, and what it cannot</h2>
		</div>
		<div class="hp-can">
			<div class="yes dh-rise"><b>What it can do</b><p>Research on humour styles, humour preferences and personality can reveal patterns at group level. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q1">Martin et al., 2003</a>; <a href="#q2">Ruch et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#q4">Plessen et al., 2020</a>)</span> It can help develop questions and discuss individual differences more effectively.</p></div>
			<div class="no dh-rise"><b>What it cannot do</b><p>It cannot reliably predict which joke an individual will find funny in a specific situation. A questionnaire, too, only captures the traits and behaviours it was developed and tested for.</p></div>
		</div>
	</section>

